  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="xirr-content max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-8 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      
      <div>
        <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          XIRR Calculator – Extended Internal Rate of Return
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
          The <strong>XIRR Calculator</strong> (Extended Internal Rate of Return) calculates the true annualized return for series of irregular, lump sum, or recurring investment cash flows. Unlike standard CAGR, which only considers the initial and final values of a lump sum, XIRR accurately accounts for the exact dates when each installment or redemption occurs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What is an XIRR Calculator?</h2>
      <p>
        In personal finance, most real-world investments do not happen as a single one-off deposit. Mutual fund SIPs, dividend reinvestments, periodic top-ups, ULIP installments, and systematic withdrawals occur across multiple distinct calendar dates.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because cash invested at the beginning of year 1 has much more time to compound than cash invested in year 3, a standard average return or point-to-point formula gives a distorted picture. <strong>XIRR</strong> solves this by finding the exact discount rate at which the Net Present Value (NPV) of all cash outflows (investments) equals the Net Present Value of all cash inflows (maturity or current portfolio valuation).
      </p>

      <h2>How does XIRR Work? The Mathematical Formula</h2>
      <p>
        XIRR represents the discount rate <em>r</em> that satisfies the Net Present Value (NPV) equation using actual day differences:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 overflow-x-auto my-3 text-center">
        &Sigma; [ C<sub>i</sub> / (1 + <var>r</var>)<sup>(D<sub>i</sub> - D<sub>0</sub>) / 365</sup> ] = 0
      </div>
      <p>Where:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>C<sub>i</sub>:</strong> Cash flow amount on date <em>i</em>. Outflows (investments) are negative, and inflows (maturity value or current valuation) are positive.</li>
        <li><strong>D<sub>i</sub>:</strong> The calendar date of cash flow <em>i</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>D<sub>0</sub>:</strong> The calendar date of the initial investment.</li>
        <li><strong>(D<sub>i</sub> - D<sub>0</sub>) / 365:</strong> The exact elapsed duration fraction in years based on actual calendar days.</li>
        <li><strong>r:</strong> The annualized Extended Internal Rate of Return (XIRR).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>XIRR vs. CAGR vs. Absolute Return: Key Differences</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 text-slate-900 dark:text-white font-bold">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Metric</th>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">When to Use</th>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Cash Flow Support</th>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Considers Dates?</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 dark:divide-slate-800 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Absolute Return</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Short-term investments (&lt; 1 year)</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Single lump sum</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">No (ignores duration)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">CAGR</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">One-time lump sum investment (&gt; 1 year)</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Only initial &amp; final value</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Only total years</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">XIRR</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">SIPs, multiple deposits, irregular cash flows</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Multiple inflows &amp; outflows</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Yes (exact calendar dates)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Step-by-Step Example Calculation</h2>
      <p>
        Consider an investor making three annual installments of ₹10,000 each into an equity fund:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>01/01/2021:</strong> First installment of <strong>-₹10,000</strong></li>
        <li><strong>01/01/2022:</strong> Second installment of <strong>-₹10,000</strong> (Day 365)</li>
        <li><strong>01/01/2023:</strong> Third installment of <strong>-₹10,000</strong> (Day 730)</li>
        <li><strong>01/01/2024:</strong> Redemption / Maturity of <strong>+₹60,000</strong> (Day 1,095)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Total invested capital is ₹30,000, and the maturity value is ₹60,000. Applying the XIRR root-finding equation:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 overflow-x-auto my-3 text-center">
        -10,000 - [ 10,000 / (1 + <var>r</var>)<sup>1</sup> ] - [ 10,000 / (1 + <var>r</var>)<sup>2</sup> ] + [ 60,000 / (1 + <var>r</var>)<sup>3</sup> ] = 0
      </div>
      <p>
        Solving for <em>r</em> yields <em>r &approx; 0.38919</em>, or an <strong>XIRR of 38.92% p.a.</strong> Note that a naive CAGR calculation using just ₹30,000 over 3 years would yield only 25.99%, significantly understating the actual compounding power of the latter installments!
      </p>

      <h2>Why Actual Investment Dates Matter</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike theoretical financial models that assume each month has exactly 30.416 days or that every year has 365 days, actual financial returns depend on exact banking dates:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Calendar Discrepancies:</strong> Leap years (such as 2024) have 366 days. February has 28 or 29 days, while July and August both have 31 days.</li>
        <li><strong>Weekend &amp; Holiday Shifts:</strong> In real mutual funds, if an SIP falls on a Saturday, Sunday, or market holiday, the NAV allocation happens on the next business day.</li>
        <li><strong>Unequal Holding Periods:</strong> Money invested on January 1 has 365 days to grow, whereas money invested on December 1 has only 31 days. XIRR discounts each installment by its exact calendar duration.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How Recurring Investment Frequencies Affect Cash Flows</h2>
      <p>
        Our calculator supports five standard recurring investment intervals:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>14 Days:</strong> Popular for bi-weekly payroll deduction programs and high-frequency equity savings.</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> The standard SIP (Systematic Investment Plan) schedule used across mutual funds.</li>
        <li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> Standard frequency for corporate dividends and government small savings schemes (such as NSC and PPF interest).</li>
        <li><strong>Half Yearly:</strong> Traditional frequency for fixed deposit interest payouts and bond coupon payments.</li>
        <li><strong>Yearly:</strong> Standard interval for tax-saving investments (ELSS, PPF annual contributions, life insurance premiums).</li>
      </ul>

