      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">What is the difference between XIRR and IRR?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            IRR assumes that all cash flows occur at strictly equal time intervals (e.g., exactly 1 year apart). XIRR allows each cash flow to occur on any arbitrary calendar date and calculates the exact day difference, making it suitable for real-world investments.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Why can't I use CAGR to evaluate my mutual fund SIP?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            CAGR only considers the first investment date and the final value. In an SIP, you invest fresh capital each month, so each installment has a different holding period. Applying CAGR to an SIP ignores the staggered nature of your capital and produces inaccurate results.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Can XIRR be negative?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            Yes. If the total maturity or current valuation of your investment is less than the cumulative capital invested, the XIRR will be negative, reflecting an annualized portfolio loss.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Does the maturity date include another recurring investment?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            No. Our calculator strictly halts recurring investments once the schedule reaches the maturity date. On the maturity date, only the positive redemption cash flow is recorded.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
